    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring test: given that this election produces a parliament first and a government only afterward through negotiation, does this campaign choice build a viable path to a governing majority. Personalisierte Verhältniswahl and the 5% threshold set the basic electoral math every campaign has to solve, and the Leihstimmenkampagne is the specific tactic smaller coalition partners use to exploit that same two-vote structure. Parteienfinanzierung and the regulated broadcast advertising regime both flow from the same constitutional Chancengleichheit principle, and together shape what resources are actually available and how a campaign can spend them. Koalitionssignale is the strategic layer every serious party has to manage on top of its own platform, the Triell debates are where that signaling most visibly plays out on camera, and the Kanzlerbonus is the structural asymmetry between an incumbent and a challenger that sits underneath all of it. Landtagswahlen supply the running scoreboard strategists actually use to read the federal mood between elections, and the AfD's rise, the BSW's emergence, and the Brandmauer strategy built in response are the single biggest live force reshaping every other concept in this lecture at once. As you follow any real German campaign from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and ask at each step whether the tactic you are watching is actually building toward a workable governing majority or simply maximizing a vote share that may not convert into power at all.",
